  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 948 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAn elongated upper-level ridge stretches from the Plains to the \nCarolinas. On the west edge of the high, a plume of mid-level \nmoisture from the Gulf of California will increase shower and storm \ncoverage for the region this afternoon and evening. The most recent \nGFS and ECMWF runs have lowland PWAT values running between 1.3-\n1.4\". Southwestern New Mexico and the upper elevations have the best \nchance of seeing scattered thunderstorms this afternoon. Some \nlingering clouds from overnight showers in southwestern New Mexico \ncould slow the start of lowland isolated thunderstorms until later \nin the afternoon. Localized flooding and gusty outflow winds are the \nmain hazards with these storms. The past few HRRR runs have been \nconsistent with forecasting a large outflow with 50 mph gusts \nblowing in from Chihuahua around sunset. To reflect this, I put a \nmention of areas of patchy blowing dust for much of the lowlands \nfrom 6-9 PM this evening, including the El Paso and Las Cruces metro \nareas.\n\nMoisture will increase on Friday, and both the GFS and the ECMWF \nhave lowland PWAT values ranging between 1.4-1.5\". The WPC has a \nSlight Risk of Excessive Rainfall for the Gila National Forest and \nareas west of the Continental Divide, while the rest of the region \nhas a Marginal Risk for Friday. El Paso and Hudspeth Counties have a \n30-50% chance to see storms in the afternoon/evening hours on \nFriday. Like Thursday, localized flooding and gusty winds will be \nthe main hazards with these storms. On Saturday, the upper-level \nhigh pressure system will begin to move back over the high plains.\nThere will be enough moisture to continue the streak of isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms, though the mountains will be favored over \nthe lowlands.\n\nPWAT values are expected decrease to 1.2-1.25\" on Sunday if the \nlatest GFS and ECMWF are any indication. This is enough moisture to \nfuel scattered mountain thunderstorms and isolated lowland \nthunderstorms, though coverage will be favored west of the \nContinental Divide. Monday and Tuesday's storm chances are currently \nexpected to be similar to Sunday.  \n\nThe long-range model runs begin to diverge in terms of rain chances \nbeyond Wednesday. The GFS has the upper-level high stationed over \nthe Southeast, which would allow for moisture to move into the \nregion by mid-next week. The ECMWF on the other hand has a center of \nhigh pressure over Arizona/New Mexico, which would limit convection. \nI'm leaning toward the ECMWF's forecast, though that may change as \nsubsequent model runs begin to converge.\n\nTemperatures for the next week will continue \nto run about 3-7 degrees above average for most of the region, with \nlowland high temperatures consistently in the mid 90s.",
